[1707.341, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ All 20 levels check out as solvable. The checker only parses the game code, so I'll also run it against a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  stubbed browser and simulate play to catch runtime errors.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1710.314, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd <workspace> && cat > smoke.js <<'E')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1715.638, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I pushed Mirror Twins further. It now has 20 levels (up from 10), a new plate-and-gate mechanic, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  animation, sound and a win screen. I haven't played it in a real browser. The solver confirms all 20 levels\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  are solvable, and a scripted run against a stubbed browser, with simulated key presses, ran without errors.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **New mechanic:** green plates (`p`) open green gates (`=`) while a twin or crate rests on them. A gate\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  can't close on anything, so a move that would do that is refused. The solver handles these rules too.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Levels:** I wrote a generator that built thousands of random boards and kept only those the solver rated\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hard. For the gate levels I dropped boards where the gate didn't matter. Levels 11–20 climb from a shortest\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  solution of 12 moves to 32, mixing gate boards with plain ones. I also had a 12-level plan that came out as\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  10, because the generator found no board for two of the planned slots.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Polish:** twins and crates now slide smoothly, goal rings pulse, and a twin glows when it sits on its\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  goal. Moves, undo, spikes and winning have small sounds. Solving a level shows a 1–3 star overlay (3 stars\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  means par or better), and Enter goes to the next level.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I didn't hand-tune the generated levels, so some may feel more arbitrary than the first ten. Tell me which\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ones feel off and I can regenerate them. Other directions I could take: a level editor, teleporters, or a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  daily-puzzle mode.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The game is `<workspace>/mirror-twins/index.html`. The generator and checking scripts are in `<workspace>`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
